, "baseline_margin": 5.59, "analyst_adjustment": -0.39, "key_factors": ["Prediction markets price Mendoza around 82-89% to win", "Most expert raters moved the seat to Lean or Likely D for a flip", "Strong Democratic-leaning national environment in the fundamentals", "Ciscomani's incumbency and cash edge partially offset the tilt"], "rationale": "Markets and most raters treat this as a Democratic flip, and the favorable environment outweighs Ciscomani's incumbency advantage. The sparse, dated polls show only a narrow Mendoza lead, so the margin stays modest rather than runaway.", "watch": "Any fresh independent polling showing Ciscomani competitive, or a strong incumbent debate performance, could pull this back toward tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Juan Ciscomani (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's 6th is an even-partisanship swing seat where first-term Republican Juan Ciscomani faces Democrat JoAnna Mendoza. Mendoza has outraised the incumbent and holds a solid cash position, while outside spending on both sides has been minimal. Three polls, the newest from June, each show Mendoza narrowly ahead, though all come from partisan sponsors and are months old.\n\nThe expert consensus has drifted toward Democrats, with several raters calling the seat Likely D for a flip and others calling it a tossup. Prediction markets price Mendoza's chances in the low-to-mid 80s. Ciscomani retains incumbency, a cash edge, and a record of bipartisan work, but the broader environment favors Democrats, leaving the race leaning their way with real uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8163, "margin": 5.47}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8157, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8181, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8226, "margin": 5.6}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7652, "margin": 5.2}]}